  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 309 PM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nAs we head into the middle of the week, the area grows in the Day 3 \nERO for Wednesday to include most of South Florida from the Tamiami \nTrail/US 41 corridor northward. The trough that had helped propel \nthe frontal boundary south early in the week will eject into the \nAtlantic which will cause the boundary to settle somewhere over the \ncentral to south central peninsula of Florida. Saturation from \nantecedent rainfall events, slower drainage along the coast due to \nhigher than normal high tides with the new moon, and the potential \nfor several rounds of excessive rainfall require monitoring of the \nforecast through the week.\n\nThis all stands independent of an area in the southwestern Gulf that \nthe National Hurricane Center is monitoring. This afternoon's \nTropical Weather Outlook currently has a 60% chance of development \nthrough 48 hours and an 80% chance of development through the next 7 \ndays. It is too early to know the precise impacts from this \npotential disturbance, but several guidance suites allow the frontal \nboundary to remain somewhere around the central peninsula of Florida \nwith abundant tropical moisture capable of producing excessive \nrainfall through at least late in the week. \n\nAtlantic high pressure building in, the potential development, and \nthen movement of the disturbance introduce some potential for the \nfrontal boundary to retreat northward this weekend but a plume of \nmoisture from the Caribbean could follow which could keep the rain \naround a bit longer. There is some consensus on the potential for a \npocket of drier air to move into South Florida from the east along \nwith the high shifting closer to the Bahamas for Sunday. It's still \ntoo early to take complete stock in the extended forecast, but the \ncurrent trends do appear to remain wet with the potential for a few \nwindows of excessive rainfall through the week.",
